[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Complex Data Types and Built-in Functions in Terraform"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Terraform configuration language goes beyond simple strings, numbers, and booleans. To build flexible, reusable, and dynamic infrastructure, you must master complex data types and built-in functions. This tutorial explores lists, maps, objects, and powerful functions to manipulate them."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Complex Data Types in Terraform"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Terraform provides several structural types to group data logically. Understanding how to define and traverse these types is crucial for advanced module design."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Lists and Tuples: Ordered sequences of values. Lists require all elements to be of the same type, while tuples allow mixed types."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Maps and Objects: Collections of key-value pairs. Maps require homogeneous value types, whereas objects allow heterogeneous types with specific attribute schemas."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"variable "environment_config" {
  type = object({
    name = string
    instances = number
    tags = map(string)
  })
  default = {
    name = "production"
    instances = 3
    tags = {
      Environment = "prod"
      Owner = " DevOps"
    }
  }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Essential Built-in Functions"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Terraform cannot run arbitrary programming language code, but it includes over a hundred built-in functions for string manipulation, collection processing, IP math, and encoding."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Collection Functions: Functions like element(), lookup(), distinct(), and length() help query and filter data structures."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"String Functions: Functions like format(), lower(), split(), and substr() dynamically generate naming conventions and configurations."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"locals {
  availability_zones = ["us-east-1a", "us-east-1b", "us-east-1c"]
  selected_zone = element(local.availability_zones, 0)
  upper_env = upper(var.environment_config.name)
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Advanced Manipulation with For Expressions"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Often, you need to transform a list or map into a different format. Terraform's 'for' expressions provide a concise syntax for transforming collections without external scripts."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"variable "subnet_cidrs" {
  type = list(string)
  default = ["10.0.1.0/24", "10.0.2.0/24", "10.0.3.0/24"]
}

output "subnet_map" {
  value = { for idx, cidr in var.subnet_cidrs : "subnet_${idx}" => cidr }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining complex data types, built-in functions, and 'for' expressions, you can drastically reduce code duplication and build robust infrastructure modules that adapt seamlessly to varying inputs."}]
